[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
# Milestone policy

A milestone records a readiness or approval decision for an identified
component, subsystem, interface, integrated system, compliance package, or
release baseline.

It does not select a project-wide development profile.

Use immutable UIDs with this form:

```text
MILESTONE-[AREA]-[NNN]
```

Approval applies only to the recorded baseline. When a later baseline replaces
it, create a new milestone or separately auditable superseding approval.

Every milestone must identify:

- project and technical scope
- included and excluded variants
- safety relevance
- exact configuration baseline
- interface revisions and compatibility constraints
- requirements, design, implementation, and evidence in scope
- objective exit criteria
- anomalies and limitations
- next activity authorized by approval
- required independence and approval authority.

A component milestone normally means that the component may enter the specified
integration activity. It does not establish that the complete system is
verified, validated, acceptably safe, compliant, or ready for release.
<<<

[MILESTONE]
UID: MILESTONE-TEMPLATE-COMMS-001
STATUS: Planned
TITLE: Template communications component ready for integration
SAFETY_RELEVANCE: NotSafetyRelated
GATE_TYPE: IntegrationReadiness
PROJECT: Template mixed-criticality product
COMPONENT: Template communications component
SCOPE: Functional communication behavior, storage behavior, controlled safety-boundary interface, update behavior, and identified product variants.
BASELINE: Replace with immutable software commit, build identifier, image digest, configuration version, and interface revision.
NEXT_ACTIVITY: Controlled integration with the identified receiving-system baseline.
DESCRIPTION: Confirm that the non-safety-related component satisfies its allocated requirements and is ready for controlled integration.
TARGET_DATE: 2026-12-15
EXIT_CRITERIA: >>>
- allocated functional, edge-case, and error requirements are verified
- design and code reviews are complete
- interface contracts are approved and versioned
- component tests pass for the identified baseline
- startup, shutdown, update, rollback, storage failure, and recovery are tested
- resource limits are established
- anomalies and limitations have dispositions
- required receiving-system tests are defined.
<<<
REQUIRED_EVIDENCE: >>>
- requirement and design reviews
- interface specification
- component verification report
- build provenance and configuration record
- software bill of materials
- anomaly and limitation report
- integration-test recommendations.
<<<
DEPENDENCIES: >>>
Receiving activity: Safety-related system integration
Required receiving baseline:
Interface specification and revision:
CPU, memory, storage, network, and timing assumptions:
Update and rollback assumptions:
Cybersecurity assumptions:
Required integration tests:
Change-notification authority:
<<<
LIMITATIONS: >>>
Known anomalies:
Unsupported configurations:
Operational restrictions:
Safety assumptions imposed by the receiving system:
Changes requiring renewed integration approval:
<<<
TAILORING_RATIONALE: Lightweight implementation evidence is permitted because the component does not implement a safety function. Receiving-system integration must verify containment and every relied-upon safety assumption.
INDEPENDENCE: Peer
APPROVAL_AUTHORITY: Communications component owner
APPROVAL: Replace with approver, decision, date, baseline, conditions, and retained approval reference.
